# guard-b allowlist for donmai — same grammar as the engine's own header
# (scripts/guard-b-lint.sh), three or four ' :: '-separated fields:
#
#     <RULE_ID>[,<RULE_ID>...] :: <location-regex> :: <match-regex>[ :: <line-regex>]
#
# See scripts/guard-b-lint.sh for the full grammar and refusal rules.
#
# This repo's --all tree scan currently reports a large pre-existing residue
# (disclosed in the PR that introduced this guard, and in
# scripts/guard-b-lint.sh's own CI job) that predates guard-b and was never
# curated against it — the previous scripts/leak-guard.sh this replaces used a
# narrower rule set that missed it. That residue is NOT allowlisted here: an
# allowlist entry is an asserted, reviewed "this is legitimate," and asserting
# that at bulk without reading each hit would be worse than the residue itself.
# --all therefore runs report-only in CI for now (see .github/workflows/guard-b.yml)
# until it is curated file-by-file, the same way tui-components' and
# donmai-smokes' were. New content is still fully covered: self-test,
# --commits (this PR's own commits), --stdin (the squash message), and
# --commits (what just landed on main) all run BLOCKING.
#
# NOTE ON THE [BRACKETS]: this file is itself scanned, so any entry added here
# must spell a banned literal with one bracketed letter (`Rens[e]i`,
# `/api/cl[i]/`) — the same convention scripts/guard-b-lint.sh and its own
# .guard-allowlist use.

# daemon/kit_registry.go: the parsed-manifest `Requires` struct carries a
# `[requires].rens[e]i` version-compatibility field, pre-existing and
# unrelated to any change that touches this file incidentally (e.g. adding
# an unrelated field to the neighbouring `Provide` struct) — --staged scans
# the whole file, not just the diff. Renaming the manifest-schema field is a
# breaking change to 005-kit-manifest-spec.md and every shipped kit.toml;
# out of scope here. Curated file-by-file per this header's remediation
# path, scoped to the two exact call sites so a NEW unscoped hit still fails.
BRAND_NAME :: ^daemon/kit_registry\.go :: Rens[e]i :: (toml:"rens[e]i"|m\.Requires\.Rens[e]i)

# afcli/commands.go: Config.OrgFunc/ProjectFunc's doc comments name the real
# wire headers the closed platform's CLI user-token auth reads
# (`X-Rens[e]i-Org` / `X-Rens[e]i-Project`), and BinaryName's doc comment cites
# the closed downstream repo that sets it — all pre-existing, unrelated to
# the change that first staged this file's other edits (a new Config field
# appended further down the struct); --staged scans the whole file, not
# just the diff. Rewriting the doc comments to omit the real header/repo
# names they document would make the documentation false. Scoped to the
# five exact pre-existing lines so a NEW unscoped hit still fails.
BRAND_NAME :: ^afcli/commands\.go :: Rens[e]i :: (`X-Rens[e]i-Project` header on every request|sent as `X-Rens[e]i-Org` on every|`X-Rens[e]i-Org` per invocation makes the scope authoritative|org/project scope as `X-Rens[e]i-Org` . `X-Rens[e]i-Project` headers)
CLOSED_TUI_REPO :: ^afcli/commands\.go :: rens[e]i-tui :: Embedders \(e\.g\. rens[e]i-tui\) set this to "rensei"
